## M8 — the resize heal neutered — SURVIVED (declared, with its cause)
Mutation A: the heal's guard -> `if True: return`. Mutation B (harder): the heal's call
at the end of `refresh_view` removed entirely. BOTH left
`tests/test_chainmap_app.py::test_AT_801c_a_resize_heals_the_selection_in_one_refresh`
GREEN.

Cause (traced, not guessed): the shipped `_select_first` (app.py) has a chainmap
branch — a selection not in the nav order moves to the first linked task in draw
order — and `_nav_flat()` is fold-aware, so on the NEXT refresh_view the stale
selection is already healed by shipped code. The end-state assertion after
`pilot.pause()` drains every pending refresh, so the arm observes the healed
end-state no matter which mechanism got there first. The increment's heal makes the
FIRST refresh's paint transient-free and lands the cursor on the nearest painted row
(min line_map row) instead of the first nav task — a real but transient-level
difference an end-state arm cannot isolate without pinning Textual's own refresh
churn (3 refreshes per measured resize).

Disposition: SURVIVED-with-cause, declared per the mutation-verdicts grammar (an arm
that stayed GREEN is named, and why). If the one-refresh law ever needs a paint-level
pin, that is a new arm asserting mid-repaint state — recorded here as the follow-up,
not a block.

